  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.1. Increased capacity of Member States to ratify international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 12 August 2022, the Government of China deposited the instruments of ratification of the Forced Labour Convention, 1930 (No. 29)  and the Abolition of Forced Labour Convention, 1957 (No. 105) , bringing the number of ILO Fundamental Conventions ratified by China to seven and the total number of China&#x2019;s ratified ILO Conventions to 28. With these ratifications, China reinforces its commitment to respect, promote and realize the ILO Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work.
The ratification of the forced labour Conventions has paved the way for Chinese constituents and other stakeholders to combat forced labour, and support human-centred high-quality development and decent work.  This progress also contributes to the realization of Sustainable Development Goal 8, Target 7, which calls on states to take immediate and effective measures to eradicate forced labour and end modern slavery and human trafficking.
 Over the years ILO and tripartite constituents have cooperated to realize the fundamental principle to abolish forced labour and promote the ratification of the forced labour Conventions (C. 29, C. 105 and P. 29).  
ILO training materials and tools addressing forced labour issues were translated into Chinese and disseminated through various channels. 

Several studies including gap analysis and technical workshops/consultations conducted to assist China in improving the legal framework towards the ratification of the forced labour Conventions.

Furthermore, ILO prepared and shared the technical Technical Memorandum concerning Office comments on the &#x201C;Gap Analysis &#x2013; Promotion of the Ratification of the Forced  Labour Conventions; organised technical consultation on application of C.29 and C.105 on 17 August 2022; a capacity building workshop on C.29 and C.105 organised from 15 to 16 August 2023 for government authorities and social partners at national and local levels to enforce compliance with national laws and Conventions against forced labour; and a knowledge sharing forum among countries held in October 2023 to share country experiences and practices in implementation of C.29 and C.105.</narrative>
   </description>
